WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.370
- Such a fan, man.

00:00:02.417 --> 00:00:03.870
Well, I asked everybody to bring, like,

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:04.703
their favorite books.

00:00:04.703 --> 00:00:06.680
I know it's very hard
to pick a favorite book,

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:07.640
but what did you bring?

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.860
- You know, for me, when
you don't have money,

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.760
books are a passport to the world, right?

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.500
They're like, they're this
gift that let you see yourself

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.060
in new ways and they open
you to new possibilities,

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.820
and I didn't have books in my house.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:20.790
We had, as you, I had the library card.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:21.980
So, these were my two library books.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.560
My first two library books.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.290
I brought "Tales of the Fourth
Grade Nothing" by Judy Blume.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:27.123
- Yeah.
- Right?

00:00:27.123 --> 00:00:28.570
'Cause I was like, this
gave me my love of reading.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:29.510
- Judy Blume is a hit today.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:30.680
- She's hit, and by the way,

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:31.920
I almost took "Are You There, God?

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:32.753
It's Me, Margaret,"

00:00:32.753 --> 00:00:33.586
'cause I was like the one boy

00:00:33.586 --> 00:00:35.690
who wanted to know how bras
worked and everything was on,

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:36.690
you know.
- You know what?

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:38.160
Clever boy.
- I know.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.440
And then what gave my love of
writing was Agatha Christie,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.040
'cause in those first chapters,

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.970
there was that question
I've asked myself forever,

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:45.010
which is it's a dead body,

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.719
and I wanna know whodunnit.

00:00:46.719 --> 00:00:47.552
- Oh my God.
- That book changed my life.

00:00:47.552 --> 00:00:48.670
- You've always been like that.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.580
- I've always been obsessed
with dead people, yes.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.110
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.400
- That says a lot.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.810
Well, I hear you love storytelling

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.530
before you even like read books, right?

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:59.130
- Yeah, so my grandfather gave
me my love of storytelling.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:02.348
This was a story that my
grandfather always told me,

00:01:02.348 --> 00:01:03.671
and it would go like this.

00:01:03.671 --> 00:01:05.795
Batman and Robin are in the Batmobile,

00:01:05.795 --> 00:01:07.541
and in front of them on a cliff

00:01:07.541 --> 00:01:09.951
is a white van with
the Joker, the Penguin,

00:01:09.951 --> 00:01:11.844
the Riddler, and Catwoman,

00:01:11.844 --> 00:01:15.442
and then they caught him, and
I would go, "Tell it again."

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:16.682
And he would go Batman and
Robin are in the Batmobile.

00:01:16.682 --> 00:01:18.220
It was like 30 words in that story.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.053
- Yeah.

00:01:19.053 --> 00:01:19.886
- But I loved it,

00:01:19.886 --> 00:01:21.090
and I love my grandfather for realizing

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.750
that I love storytelling.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.010
It means so much to me.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.250
- Oh, I do that with my kids.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:26.760
It's so funny, too, what kids will come up

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.700
when you ask your kids to tell a story.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.480
First of all, it's a good stall tactic,

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.290
'cause they'll try and, like,
make their stories so long

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:33.430
they never have to go to bed.
- My whole life.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.460
- Yeah, but it is interesting to see

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:36.780
what they come up with, as well.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.150
- And in my house, trust me,

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.390
my kids are also critics
because I write books,

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:42.223
so my daughter's like-

00:01:42.223 --> 00:01:43.056
- They're all critics.

00:01:43.056 --> 00:01:43.980
- That's not that good, Dad.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.140
You need more razzmatazz.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.610
I'm like, are you from the 1920s?

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:47.890
Razzmatazz?
- Razzmatazz?

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:48.990
- I'm like, what is that?

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.340
- So, you're best known

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:51.680
as a writer of mysteries and thrillers,

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.480
so how did you start writing kids books?

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.890
- Yeah, you know what,
much like you, I had kids,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.390
and I wanted to write and give them

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:58.970
better heroes to look up to.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.350
I was tired of them looking at people

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:01.530
who were famous being famous.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.180
I said, how do I teach 'em kindness?

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:04.410
How do I teach 'em perseverance?

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.060
How to be a good person?

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.920
So, for perseverance, I
did "I'm Amelia Earhart,"

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.140
the little girl who
was unstoppable, right?

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.160
To teach creativity, I
did " I Am Walt Disney"

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.750
who used his creativity
to change the world.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.537
To teach them how to fight back,

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:20.130
"I am Rosa Parks," "I Am
Martin Luther King, Jr."

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.160
Chris Eliopoulos and
I, our amazing artist,

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.700
just decided this is the best thing,

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.380
and right now I looked at
what my kids need right now,

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:27.820
and they're anxious.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.050
We're all anxious.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.000
And so I said, we need to do
our first book on self-love,

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.840
so we did "I Am Oprah
Winfrey" to teach the lesson,

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.180
the lesson is in the book,

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.730
that if you wanna live your best life,

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.310
you gotta be your true self.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:40.551
And we all need that lesson right now.

00:02:40.551 --> 00:02:41.384
(audience applauds)

00:02:41.384 --> 00:02:43.870
- I love that the books
tell the backstory.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.700
I love that it tells a
bit of the backstory,

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:46.533
how they got there,

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:48.430
'cause we always see these
people as like, oh man,

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:49.370
they have this, they have this,

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:50.870
and you don't think of,
like, what got them there.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:51.840
I think that's really cool.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.040
- That that's the real
secret of it, right?

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.927
We always show them when they're kids

00:02:54.927 --> 00:02:57.130
and we always show them
when they fail, right?

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.350
'Cause everyone you look up to,

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:01.370
whether it is Amelia Earhart
or Rosa Parks or Dr. King,

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:03.340
they had moments where they
were scared and terrified.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.600
They didn't know if they could go on.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:05.870
- Yeah.
- But they do.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:06.703
And Oprah Winfrey,

00:03:06.703 --> 00:03:07.640
best story's, right?

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.000
When she's three years old in church,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.310
she finds the power of her voice.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.260
When she's a little girl,
she hates the way she looks.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.750
She sleeps with a clothespin on her nose

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:19.590
to make her nose smaller.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:22.200
Her producers tell her her
body's not the right type,

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.660
her skin is too dark,

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.640
and they tell her that she
cares too much about people.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:27.760
She finds, this is a true story.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:28.810
Can't make this up.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:29.713
She's reporting on this story.

00:03:29.713 --> 00:03:30.630
- I believe that last part.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:31.463
- I know you believe this, right?

00:03:31.463 --> 00:03:32.296
- 20 years in this business.

00:03:32.296 --> 00:03:33.320
- And they tell her,

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:34.930
she goes to cover a story about

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.050
a family's house who burns down,

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.240
and she, the next day, privately,

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.060
goes and gives the family blankets,

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.257
and the producer yells at her and says,

00:03:42.257 --> 00:03:43.900
"You can't get involved in this story,"

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:45.080
and she says, "The story?

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:47.020
What about being a good human being?"

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:48.720
- Amen.
- And I want my daughter

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:49.553
to learn that lesson.

00:03:49.553 --> 00:03:50.404
No, I appreciate that.

00:03:50.404 --> 00:03:51.237
Thank you.

00:03:51.237 --> 00:03:54.010
I mean, but I want my
daughter to know that, like,

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.210
they told Oprah your
body's not the right size.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:57.550
You don't look the right way.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:58.560
- [Kelly] She defied the odds.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.190
- You can't care, and
there's no such thing.

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.570
The only person you have to be is you.

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:05.480
That is the greatest
gift you give yourself.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:06.313
- Yeah.

00:04:06.313 --> 00:04:07.570
What I love about it specifically

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:09.310
is my kids are obsessed
with, like, superheroes,

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:10.640
especially my five-year-old boy,

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:11.610
and so I am, too.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:12.603
I'm 39, whatever.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.850
But I love the idea, though,

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.420
that these are superheroes as well.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:18.420
I love that series.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:19.610
So, my kids have this series.

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:20.443
- I appreciate it.

00:04:20.443 --> 00:04:21.290
- It's amazing, though,

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:22.980
to be able to teach them,
first of all, history.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.260
You're teaching so much at once

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:26.297
without them really even realizing it.

00:04:26.297 --> 00:04:27.130
- Yeah, oh, then, that's right,

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:28.280
but they are superheroes.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.113
They are.

00:04:29.113 --> 00:04:30.800
I mean, I will put Rosa Parks again,

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.430
tell me that's not Wonder Woman, right?

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:34.280
Tell me that Abraham
Lincoln is not Superman.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.430
- But we don't teach it like that.

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:38.040
- Right, what we do with our heroes

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.580
is we put them on pedestals
and we make them perfect,

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.120
and we do our heroes a real disservice

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.800
'cause they're all human
beings, and, you know,

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.950
the series is called "Ordinary
People Change the World,"

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:47.980
and everyone's like,
oh, they're so amazing.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:50.150
I'm like, no, no, they all, as I said

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.660
have moments where they don't
think they can do it anymore,

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.320
and we're all ordinary people.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.390
I don't care where you went to school,

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:56.223
how much money you made.

00:04:56.223 --> 00:04:57.056
- [Kelly] John legend told us.

00:04:57.056 --> 00:04:58.680
- Right?
- Ordinary people.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:04:59.513
- Change the world, right?

00:04:59.513 --> 00:05:02.300
I mean, I owe him for
that one now, I think.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.040
But really, I tell it to my kids,

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.690
but I know one thing,
I'm not that special,

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:06.530
and there were parents out there

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:07.670
who want the same thing for their kids,

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:08.790
so they build these libraries

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:10.440
of real heroes for their kids

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:11.800
and their nieces and their
nephews, their grandkids.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:12.633
I love that.

00:05:12.633 --> 00:05:14.010
- It's clever, and it's
good, and it's important.

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:14.950
So, thank you for that.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:16.030
But, well, the books,

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:17.790
the books are powerful
enough that many teachers

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.720
have actually started using
them in the classrooms.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:20.553
- [Brad] Yeah.

00:05:20.553 --> 00:05:21.680
- Which is a big thing,
'cause my mom's a teacher,

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:23.410
and they usually make you
stick to that curriculum

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:24.580
of what everybody does.

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:26.880
- Yeah, I love our teachers,

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:27.760
our teachers and librarians.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.510
I mean, I've had, and of
course they'll say, like,

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:31.470
we'll do a story time, and
they'll send me a picture.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.590
They watch our story times on YouTube.

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:35.100
But what I love is
they'll show me pictures

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.390
of their kids dressed
up as Jackie Robinson,

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:38.550
and here they are is Rosa Parks,

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:40.100
and here they are is Lucille Ball,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.200
but the best one, we
had a school in Chicago,

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:46.080
took our books and literally came to me.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.400
They made a whole musical
with just our books.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.430
And there's a little Dr. King

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.250
and there's a little Jackie Robinson

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:54.360
and a little George
Washington, Abraham Lincoln,

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.710
and I'm like, you made a musical?

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:57.543
- [Kelly] That's brilliant.

00:05:57.543 --> 00:05:58.800
- I want tickets to this now.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:05:59.633
Like, I was like, why
didn't you invite us?

00:05:59.633 --> 00:06:01.780
- Hey, that's actually a really good idea.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:04.600
Like, on Broadway, you should
one-word, one-third that, man.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:06.120
I'm just saying, that's a great idea.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:07.390
- So you get points on the
backed for that, I'm guessing.

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:09.750
- I'm just saying that's a
really smart, clever idea,

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:10.960
and then introducing it's like, I mean,

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:12.240
it's kind of what they did with Hamilton.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:13.980
They used all these historical characters.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:14.813
Like, you know what I'm saying?

00:06:14.813 --> 00:06:15.950
But to do that in such a bigger,

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:17.270
broader way with all the, anyway.

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:18.313
We'll talk after.
- When I went to see Hamilton,

00:06:18.313 --> 00:06:19.850
I literally brought the
George Washington book

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:20.950
and I gave it to Chris Jackson.

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:22.340
I was like, you're my George Washington.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:23.173
So, yes.

00:06:23.173 --> 00:06:24.006
- Oh my gosh.

00:06:24.006 --> 00:06:25.160
I love that. I love that.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:27.670
Well, that's why this next
part is so unbelievable

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:29.300
and also infuriating.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:31.060
Two of your books were banned

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.470
by a school district in Pennsylvania.

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:34.900
Children's books.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:36.210
- I mean...
- Two children's books,

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.470
but I'm like, what year is it?

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:40.230
- Right, and you should see my house.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:41.410
So, I'm on Twitter,

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:43.097
and all of a sudden someone
tells me, they're like,

00:06:43.097 --> 00:06:45.200
"Brad, they banned 'I Am Rosa Parks'

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:46.740
and 'I Am Martin Luther King, Jr.'"

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.030
I'm like, you know, I mean, look at these.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:52.010
This is clearly trying to,
like, do some harm here, right?

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:54.210
- Clearly you're trying
to just destroy villages.

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:56.000
- My wife was so pissed.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.380
My wife is like, I'm gonna kill him,

00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:59.680
and I'm like, but I
was heartbroken, right?

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:00.513
She's murderer, right?

00:07:00.513 --> 00:07:01.346
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:07:01.346 --> 00:07:02.179
- You know our house now.

00:07:02.179 --> 00:07:04.100
- That's a good yin and yang
you have going on there.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:05.860
- But the truth was I was heartbroken

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:07.290
'cause I was like,
there are all these kids

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.270
that don't get that story now,

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:11.940
and when you look at that,
I'm like, that's just crazy,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:12.920
'cause I don't care where,

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:15.160
I know race is a hard subject, but-

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.330
- But when you shield them
from it, nothing progresses.

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:18.163
- God bless. Right?

00:07:18.163 --> 00:07:19.610
I mean, I tell my kids all the time,

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:20.500
I know it may be hard.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:21.440
You don't shy away from it.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:23.130
The only way is you gotta go through,

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.180
and we do our kids a huge disservice

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:26.610
if we don't discuss these issues.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:27.550
- And we do ourselves,

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:29.260
because the next generation,
you're not helping them.

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:30.093
Yeah.
- That's it.

00:07:30.093 --> 00:07:32.090
And these books are literally designed

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:33.110
to give your kids these lessons.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:35.150
I have, you know, in
the back of every book

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:36.670
there's a real photograph of the person.

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:38.620
So, you see this cartoon story.

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:40.490
And my friend told me this story.

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:43.690
So, he's white, his
daughter's black, adopted,

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:46.590
and he says he gets to the
last page of "I Am Rosa Parks,"

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:47.423
and he opens it,

00:07:47.423 --> 00:07:49.720
and she sees the real
picture of Rosa Parks,

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:51.070
and she says, "Wait a minute.

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:52.530
This really happened.

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:53.900
This really happened."

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:54.733
And he said,

00:07:54.733 --> 00:07:56.310
and suddenly he was having
the first real conversation

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:57.180
about race with his daughter.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:58.970
He's like, I'm from a mixed family.

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:00.460
I know I should have this,

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:02.950
but this is the book
that they banned, Kelly.

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:04.990
And I'm like, you must be kidding me.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:06.300
So, the first thing we did, of course,

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.480
is we got in touch with local teachers.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:10.860
We got in touch with, who
are incredible, by the way.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:11.940
No one fights like a teacher.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:13.080
- Yeah.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.180
You haven't met my momma.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:15.013
- No, trust me.

00:08:15.013 --> 00:08:16.150
We have similar moms, right?

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:17.033
- She's a Leo.

00:08:18.672 --> 00:08:19.780
- And what you know we did

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:21.120
is we did what Rosa Parks taught us to do

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:22.270
and what Dr. King taught us do,

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:23.220
is protest.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:25.120
We said, "Everyone,
all the books on this,"

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:28.960
not just our books, books
by Malala, Sonia Sotomayor,

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.100
the "Sesame Street" book,
they banned, our books.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:33.010
We said, "Go buy them all and protest."

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:34.407
There were 5,000 kids in that district,

00:08:34.407 --> 00:08:37.500
and my goal, I want every banned book

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:39.120
for every kid in that district.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.970
Fight back like Rosa Parks taught us.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:42.789
- Yeah.
(audience cheering)

00:08:42.789 --> 00:08:46.370
And it's such a funny, tricky thing, too,

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:48.300
especially with certain age kids.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:49.400
We'll mention puberty.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:50.750
Is if you make it a red button,

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.450
I'm like, well, what's in that book?

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:53.920
I'm like, you know what I'm saying?

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:55.260
If I'm a kid, and you've banned the book,

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.602
I'm like now I wanna know what's in it.

00:08:57.602 --> 00:08:58.435
- And who doesn't?

00:08:58.435 --> 00:08:59.300
The moment you take it away

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:01.760
that publisher's like, you
realize the sales just went up?

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:03.190
And I'm like, are we rooting

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:04.890
for all the books to be banned now?

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:05.890
What should we do?
- No, but it's one of

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:07.600
those things where it's like, why?

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:09.420
Like, and it's good to question.

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:10.253
It's good to do all.

00:09:10.253 --> 00:09:11.747
I think it's so important.

